{"item_id": "atlas_test_00165", "reference_source": "ruling:N290182", "product": "a crankshaft sensing blade used in spark ignition internal combustion piston engines", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N290182, gold code 8409.91.5085:\ngs, Part Numbers 78-02, 80-43, 80-07, 78-62, and 80-17, which are used with automotive engines. The main function of each drain plug is to seal the oil pan of an automobile engine, and to facilitate the draining and the changing of the engine oil. Parts 78-02 and 80-43 are made of 100 percent low carbon steel, while 80-07, 78-62, and 80-17 are made of 99 percent low carbon steel and 1 percent or less of rubber. With the exception of part 80-17, all oil pan drain plugs are used with spark ignition automotive engines. Part 80-17 may be used on either spark ignition or diesel automotive engines. The applicable subheading for the Oil Pan Drain Plugs (Part Numbers 78-02, 80-43, 80-07, and 78-62) will be 8409.91.5085, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Parts suitable for use solely or principally with the engines of heading 8407 or 8408: Other: Suitable for use solely or principally with spark-ignition internal combustion piston engines including rotary engines): Other: For vehicles of subheading 8701.20, or heading 8702, 8703 or 8704: Other: Other.” The general rate of duty will be 2.5%. The applicable subheading for the Oil Pan Drain Plug (Part Number 80-17) will be 8409.99.9190, HTSUS, which provides for “Parts suitable for use solely or principally with the engines of heading 8407 or 8408: Other: Other: Other: For vehicles of subheading 8701.20, or heading 8702, 8703 or 8704: Other.” The general rate of duty will be 2.5%. The applicable classification subheading duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States and the accompanying duty rates are provided on the World Wide Web at http://www.usitc.gov/current. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Liana Alvarez at liana.alvarez@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, Steven A. Mack Director National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8409.91.99.90", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: the blade is not itself a good of a specific chapter 84/85 heading (it transmits no torque, so not 8483)", "Section XVI note 2(b): a part suitable for use solely or principally with engines of heading 8407 is classified with those engines in heading 8409", "GRI 6: 8409.91 parts for spark-ignition engines; 8409.91.99 'Other' because installation in vehicles of 8701/8702-8704 or marine propulsion engines is not stated; suffix .90 other (not connecting rods)"], "rationale": "A crankshaft sensing blade is dedicated to spark-ignition piston engines of heading 8407 and is not a good named in its own chapter 84/85 heading, so Section XVI note 2(b) sends it to 8409.91. Without a stated vehicle or marine application it falls in the residual 8409.91.99.90, as with the small-engine pushrod in NY F88714 (in force). NY N270502 (in force) instead classified a similar iron sensor wheel as an article of iron in 7326.90.8588, which is a real alternative risk; if the blade is for a passenger vehicle engine the line would be 8409.91.50.85.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8409.91.50.85", "reason": "applies only if the engine is to be installed in vehicles of 8701.21-8701.29, 8702, 8703 or 8704; not stated"}, {"code": "8483.90", "reason": "the blade does not transmit power or torque (as noted in N270502)"}, {"code": "7326.90.86", "reason": "N270502 placed an iron crankshaft position sensor wheel in 7326, but Section XVI note 2(b) and heading 8409 more specifically cover a part identifiable as solely for spark-ignition engines; followed the parts provision"}, {"code": "8511.90", "reason": "the blade is not part of ignition equipment itself; it is a mechanical engine component"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00168", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a 2 pc Sleep-n-Play Set with Bib for infants, including a bodysuit, a stretchsuit, and a bib made of finely knit cotton fabric", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 6111.20.6020):\nThe product in question is a 2 pc Sleep-n-Play Set with Bib designed for infants, which includes three components: a bodysuit, a stretchsuit, and a bib. All items are constructed from finely knit cotton fabric, which is a key factor in determining the appropriate HTS classification. According to the Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), the relevant subheading for infants' garments and clothing accessories that are knitted or crocheted and made of cotton is 6111.20.6020. This classification specifically covers \"babies garments and clothing accessories, knitted or crocheted: of cotton: other: other, sets.\" The ruling confirms that the items are capable of being worn together, reinforcing their classification as a set. The duty rate for this classification is 8.1 percent ad valorem, and the textile category number is 239, which is relevant for quota and visa requirements. Thus, the HTS US code 6111.20.6020 is appropriate for this product.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6111.20.60.20", "deciding_gri": "GRI 6", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 6111 covers babies' garments and clothing accessories, knitted; chapter 61 note 6(b) gives 6111 priority over other chapter 61 headings", "Additional U.S. Note 1 to chapter 61 (as quoted in N356719): 'sets' are two or more different garments of 6111 imported together, of corresponding sizes, intended to be worn together by the same person; bodysuit, stretchsuit and bib are marketed as one outfit", "GRI 6: 6111.20 of cotton; 6111.20.10-.50 exclude goods imported as parts of sets, so 6111.20.60; statistical suffix .20 Sets (239)"], "rationale": "Infant garments of knitted cotton fall in heading 6111 under GRI 1 and chapter 61 note 6. The item is sold as a coordinated set of different babies' garments; under Additional U.S. Note 1 to chapter 61 (quoted in NY N356719, in force) such sets fall outside the 'except parts of sets' lines and go to 6111.20.60, statistical line Sets (.20). If CBP found the pieces are not worn together, each would be classified separately as in N014992 (bodysuit at 6111.20.6010, bib at 6111.20.6070).", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6111.20.60.10", "reason": "line for bodysuits/washsuits when entered individually; applies if the pieces do not meet the Additional U.S. Note 1 set definition (then bodysuit and stretchsuit each here, bib in 6111.20.60.70)"}, {"code": "6111.20.60.70", "reason": "bib alone as an accessory; used only if the items are not a set"}, {"code": "6114.20", "reason": "chapter 61 note 6(b) gives heading 6111 priority for babies' garments"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00170", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "parts of beer dispensing equipment, including a valve head, coupling head, stout faucet, and spare parts for stout faucets", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8481.90.5000):\nThe articles in question are components of beer dispensing systems, specifically including a valve head, coupling head, stout faucet, and various spare parts for stout faucets. The initial classification proposed by the user was under HTS subheading 8418, which pertains to beer dispensing equipment. However, upon review, it was determined that these items are more accurately classified under HTS heading 8481, which covers parts of taps and similar appliances. The specific subheading 8481.90.5000 applies to parts made of materials other than copper or iron/steel, which is appropriate if the materials of the parts are unspecified or fall outside those categories. The ruling confirms that the rate of duty for this classification is 3 percent ad valorem, aligning with the nature of the products as parts of hand-operated taps and valves.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8481.80.10.50", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: first-named article (valve head) is itself a valve/tap of heading 8481 ('taps, cocks, valves and similar appliances for pipes, tanks, vats or the like')", "Section XVI note 2(a): a part that is a good of a chapter 84 heading (8481) is classified in its own heading, not as a part of the dispensing machine", "GRI 6: 8481.80 other appliances; hand operated; of copper (assumed brass) 8481.80.10; pressure rating under 850 kPa; not bath, sink or supply stop, so suffix .50 other"], "rationale": "The description lists several articles; the first-named valve head is a complete hand-operated valve and so is a good of heading 8481, which Section XVI note 2(a) keeps in its own heading rather than as a part of beer dispensing machinery. NY H89948 (in force) shows CBP treating beer-tap hardware under heading 8481, split by copper or iron/steel material. Assuming brass and a working pressure under 850 kPa, the line is 8481.80.10.50; if stainless steel, 8481.80.30.90.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8481.80.30.90", "reason": "applies if the valve head is of stainless steel rather than brass"}, {"code": "8481.90.10", "reason": "parts of copper hand-operated valves; the valve head is a complete valve, not a part of one (spare parts for stout faucets would go here)"}, {"code": "8418.99 / 8479.90", "reason": "Section XVI note 2(a) keeps goods of heading 8481 in their own heading rather than as parts of dispensing machinery"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00171", "reference_source": "ruling:L80759", "product": "a bib style apron made of 100% cotton fabric", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling L80759, gold code 6211.42.0081:\ning also forms the straps that are tied in the back of the wearer at the waist area. The apron also has two patch pockets at the wearer’s waist. Style 2115-C “Waist Apron” is a tool apron made of a 65% cotton and 35% polyester woven fabric. The garment is a rectangular-shaped half apron which measures approximately 20 inches in width by 10 inches in length. It features self-fabric capping on top that extends to tie around the wearer’s waist. It has one large pocket approximately 8 inches high divided into three sections. This item has insufficient coverage to be considered a protective apron. The applicable subheading for styles 4250-C and 4350-C will be 6211.42.0081, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for track suits, ski-suits and swimwear; other garments, women’s or girls’: of cotton, other. The duty rate will be 8.1 percent ad valorem. The applicable subheading for style 2115-C will be 6307.90.9889, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTS), which provides for other made up articles, including dress patterns: other: other: other, other: other. The duty rate will be 7 percent ad valorem. Styles 4250-C and 4350-C fall within textile category designation 359. Based upon international textile trade agreements products of China are subject to quota and the requirement of a visa. The designated textile and apparel categories and their quota and visa status are the result of international agreements that are subject to frequent renegotiations and changes. To obtain the most current information, we suggest that you check, close to the time of shipment, the Textile Status Report for Absolute Quotas, which is available at our Web site at www.cbp.gov. In addition, the designated textile and apparel categories may be subdivided into parts. If so, visa and quota requirements applicable to the subject merchandise may be affected and should also be verified at the time of shipment. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, contact National Import Specialist Kenneth Reidlinger at 646-733-3053. Sincerely, Robert B. Swierupski Director, National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6211.42.10.92", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: aprons are 'other garments' of heading 6211 (woven); chapter 62 note 9 treats garments not identifiable as men's or women's as women's", "GRI 6: 6211.42 women's or girls' of cotton; 6211.42.10 other (not recreational performance outerwear); suffix .92 other (359), since not a jumper, vest, jacket-type garment or hospital/lab apparel"], "rationale": "A bib apron is a garment of heading 6211, and being unisex it is treated as women's under chapter 62 note 9. NY G83072 (in force) classified a woven cotton bib apron as a women's other garment of cotton (then 6211.42.0081, category 359). In the current tree that falls in 6211.42.10 and the residual statistical line 6211.42.10.92, since the apron is not hospital or laboratory apparel.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6211.32", "reason": "men's line; a unisex bib apron is classified as women's under chapter 62 note 9"}, {"code": "6211.42.10.88", "reason": "reserved for apparel designed for use in hospitals, clinics, laboratories or contaminated areas"}, {"code": "6114.20", "reason": "applies only if the cotton fabric is knitted"}, {"code": "6307.90", "reason": "an apron is a garment worn on the body, covered by chapter 62 rather than other made-up articles"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00172", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a FreshWater Salt System Cartridge used in spas to generate and dispense chlorine", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8421.99.0040):\nThe FreshWater Salt System Cartridge is specifically designed to work with a FreshWater Salt System, which generates chlorine for the purification of spa water. The classification process begins by identifying the primary function of the product. While the user initially proposed classifying it under HTS Code 9019.10.2090, which pertains to parts and accessories of massage apparatus (including spas), the ruling clarifies that the cartridge is a distinct component of the FreshWater Salt System, not merely a part of the spa itself. \n\nAccording to the ruling, the FreshWater Salt System Cartridge is made of plastic and contains titanium electrodes, which are integral to its function of converting salt into chlorine. This functionality aligns more closely with the classification under HTS Code 8421.99.0040, which covers parts of machinery and apparatus for filtering or purifying water. The ruling cites previous court cases that establish that when a specific part is provided for, it should be classified as part of that specific system rather than as part of a larger whole. Therefore, the FreshWater Salt System Cartridge is correctly classified under 8421.99.0040, which is designated for parts of machinery used in filtering or purifying water, with a duty rate of free, but subject to additional tariffs for products from China as specified in Chapter 99 subheadings.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8543.30.90.90", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8543 names machines and apparatus for electrolysis (8543.30); the cartridge is the electrolytic cell that performs the electrolysis, so it is the apparatus itself, not merely a part", "Section XVI note 2(a): a good that is itself described by a heading (electrolysis apparatus) stays in that heading even if installed in a spa system", "GRI 6: 8543.30.90 other (not for printed circuits); suffix .90 other (not hydrogen electrolyzer or magnesium anode)"], "rationale": "The FreshWater salt cartridge generates chlorine by passing current through salt water, which is electrolysis, so heading 8543 (machines and apparatus for electrolysis) describes it by GRI 1. NY F89627 (in force) confirms CBP treats chlorine-producing electrolysis machines under heading 8543 (there, their components in 8543.90). With the cell itself imported, the residual statistical line 8543.30.90.90 applies; no ruling on this exact spa cartridge was found.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8421.21.00.00", "reason": "the cartridge does not filter water; its function is chemical generation of chlorine by electrolysis, which heading 8543.30 names specifically"}, {"code": "8543.90.88", "reason": "parts line; applies only if the cartridge lacks the electrodes/cell and is merely a housing or component of the electrolytic apparatus"}, {"code": "2828 / 3808.94", "reason": "the article is an apparatus, not a chemical disinfectant"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00175", "reference_source": "ruling:N257782", "product": "a floor tile made from polyvinyl chloride (PVC) measuring 11.74 x 23.74 inches and 4 millimeters thick", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N257782, gold code 3918.10.1000:\nThe tariff classification of vinyl floor tiles from China Dear Mr. Om: In your letter dated Sept 30, 2014, you requested a tariff classification ruling on behalf of your client, Home Legend, LLC. A photo was submitted in lieu of a sample. The merchandise, described both as “luxury vinyl tiles” and “luxury vinyl planks,” consists of vinyl floor tiles that are imported in the form of planks measuring 7 inches wide, 48 inches long and 4 millimeters thick. They will be imported in various simulated wood grain designs. The planks will be installed utilizing a floating click-lock technology system. The applicable subheading for the vinyl floor tiles will be 3918.10.1000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for floor coverings of plastics, whether or not self-adhesive, in rolls or in the form of tiles: of polymers of vinyl chloride: floor coverings: vinyl tile. The rate of duty will be 5.3 percent ad valorem. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided on World Wide Web at http://www.usitc.gov/tata/hts/. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, contact National Import Specialist Joan Mazzola at joan.m.mazzola@cbp.dhs.cbp. Sincerely, Gwenn Klein Kirschner Director National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3918.10.10.40", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 3918 covers floor coverings of plastics in the form of tiles; chapter 39 notes do not exclude it", "GRI 6: 3918.10 of polymers of vinyl chloride; floor coverings; 3918.10.10 vinyl tile", "GRI 6 statistical suffix: .40 other, assuming a conventional flexible (non-rigid) vinyl tile rather than a rigid solid (SPC) or rigid expanded core"], "rationale": "A PVC floor tile is a floor covering of plastics in tile form of heading 3918 and, being of polymers of vinyl chloride, falls in 3918.10.10 as vinyl tile. NY N341739 (in force) confirms CBP places PVC flooring in 3918.10.10, choosing the suffix by core construction (rigid solid core at .20). Because the core is not described, the default 'Other' suffix 3918.10.10.40 is the best guess.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3918.10.10.20", "reason": "applies only if the tile has a rigid solid polymer (e.g., SPC) core; not stated"}, {"code": "3918.10.10.30", "reason": "applies only to a rigid expanded polymer core per chapter 39 statistical note 2"}, {"code": "3918.90", "reason": "the tile is of PVC, a polymer of vinyl chloride"}, {"code": "3918.10.20", "reason": "that line covers PVC floor coverings other than vinyl tile; the article is a tile"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00177", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a High Efficiency Pressurized Dynamic Classifier used in coal burning power plants", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8474.10.0090):\nThe High Efficiency Pressurized (HEP) Dynamic Classifier is designed to separate pulverized coal particles to enhance the output of coal-burning power plants. It operates by utilizing pre-separation vanes to reject coarser particles, followed by adjustable louvers and a rotor that separates smaller and lighter coal particles from heavier ones based on their tangential velocity and air flow. This machine is classified under HTS code 8474.10.0090, which pertains to stationary sorting, screening, separating, or washing machines for earth, stone, ores, or other mineral substances in solid form (including powder or paste). The classification is justified as the HEP Dynamic Classifier performs a separation function, which aligns with the description of the machines covered under this HTS code. Additionally, the ruling confirms a duty rate of 2.9 percent ad valorem for this classification, further supporting its accuracy.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8474.10.00.90", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8474 covers machinery for sorting, screening or separating mineral substances in solid (including powder) form; pulverized coal is a mineral substance", "Section XVI note 2(a): the classifier is itself a separating machine of heading 8474 (8474.10), so it is classified as such rather than as a part of the pulverizer (8474.90)", "GRI 6: 8474.10.00 sorting, screening, separating or washing machines; suffix .90 stationary (fixed to the mill), not portable"], "rationale": "A dynamic classifier size-separates pulverized coal, a mineral substance in powder form, so it falls within heading 8474's 'machinery for sorting, screening, separating ... mineral substances'. Under Section XVI note 2(a) a machine that is itself described by 8474.10 stays there even though it works with a pulverizer. NY N252078 (in force) shows CBP applying heading 8474 to mineral size-reduction and screening equipment; no ruling on a coal classifier itself was found, so the stationary line 8474.10.00.90 is chosen with moderate confidence.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8474.90.00", "reason": "parts line; the classifier performs its own separating function named in 8474.10 (Section XVI note 2(a))"}, {"code": "8474.20.00", "reason": "the classifier does not crush or grind; the pulverizer does"}, {"code": "8421.39", "reason": "the classifier sizes solid mineral particles rather than filtering or purifying a gas; 8474 is more specific for mineral substances"}, {"code": "8479.89.95", "reason": "residual heading yields to 8474, which names separating machinery for mineral substances"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00180", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "float collar and float shoe used in downhole drilling operations", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8431.43.8090):\nThe float collar and float shoe are classified under HTSUS subheading 8431.43.8090, which pertains to parts suitable for use solely or principally with machinery for boring or sinking. The classification is based on the following reasoning:\n\n1. **Definition of Parts**: The float collar and float shoe are integral components used in downhole drilling operations. They perform essential functions such as preventing drilling mud from entering the casing, guiding the casing into the wellbore, and reducing the load on the drilling derrick or mast. This qualifies them as parts necessary for the operation of boring machinery.\n\n2. **Application of General Rules of Interpretation (GRIs)**: According to GRI 1, classification is determined by the terms of the headings and any relevant section or chapter notes. The float collar and float shoe are not merely valves (which would fall under heading 8481) but serve additional functions that align them with the machinery described in heading 8431.\n\n3. **Explanatory Notes**: The Explanatory Notes for heading 8431 include various drilling equipment, confirming that the float collar and float shoe are classified correctly as parts of boring or sinking machinery.\n\n4. **Comparison with Other Rulings**: Previous CBP rulings (e.g., NYN043049 and N249636) have classified similar drilling components under heading 8431, reinforcing the classification of the float collar and float shoe as parts of boring machinery.\n\n5. **NAFTA Considerations**: The protest regarding NAFTA preferential treatment was denied because CTE Industries did not provide the necessary amended Certificate of Origin to reflect the correct classification. This further supports the classification under 8431.43.8090 as the correct HTSUS code.\n\nIn conclusion, the float collar and float shoe are classified under HTSUS subheading 8431.43.8090 due to their integral role in drilling operations and their alignment with the definitions and examples provided in the relevant tariff headings and notes.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8431.43.80.60", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: first-named article (float collar) classified; float shoe classified the same way", "Section XVI note 2(b): parts suitable for use solely or principally with boring or sinking machinery of 8430.41/8430.49 go to heading 8431 (8431.43); Section XV note 2 does not apply since the article is not a part of general use", "GRI 6: 8431.43 parts for boring or sinking machinery; 8431.43.80 other; of oil and gas field machinery; suffix .60 other (not tool joints or drill pipe)"], "rationale": "Float collars and float shoes are downhole casing components used solely in oil and gas well drilling and cementing, so under Section XVI note 2(b) they are parts of the boring or sinking machinery of 8430.41/8430.49 in 8431.43. HQ H319171 (in force) classified stop collars and centralizers for oil well casing in 8431.43.80 and discussed an earlier HQ decision (H243821, not in the corpus, status unknown and not relied on) on float collars and shoes under heading 8431. The statistical line for other parts of oil and gas field machinery is 8431.43.80.60.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "7326.90.86", "reason": "CBP in H319171 rejected 7326 for similar casing accessories (stop collars, centralizers) that are dedicated to oil well drilling machinery"}, {"code": "8481.30", "reason": "the float collar contains a check valve, but the article as a whole is a dedicated downhole casing/drilling component, not merely a valve for pipes"}, {"code": "8431.43.80.90", "reason": "that line is for other (non-oil and gas field) boring or sinking machinery"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00181", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a DC brushless motor assembly used as a subcomponent for a laser bar code scanner", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8501.10.4040):\nThe product in question is a DC brushless motor assembly that operates at a maximum output of 1/4 watt. According to the Harmonized Tariff Schedule (HTS), electric motors are classified under subheading 8501. The specific subheading 8501.10.4040 applies to electric motors not exceeding 37.5 watts. Since the motor assembly operates well below this threshold, it falls under this classification. The ruling confirms that the applicable rate of duty for this HTS code is 6.6 percent ad valorem, which is consistent with the classification of small electric motors.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8501.10.40.40", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8501 names electric motors", "Section XVI note 2(a): a part that is itself a good of a chapter 85 heading (8501 motors) is classified in that heading, not as a part of the scanner (8471/8473)", "GRI 6: 8501.10 output not exceeding 37.5 W (assumed, small scanner motor); of under 18.65 W; not synchronous valued under $4 so 8501.10.40; DC, brushless suffix .40"], "rationale": "A DC brushless motor is named by heading 8501, and Section XVI note 2(a) keeps such a good in its own heading even when it is a subcomponent of a laser scanner. Small scanner motors have an output well under 18.65 W, placing it in 8501.10.40, DC brushless. NY H87929 (in force) classified a small DC brushless motor in the same line, 8501.10.4040.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8473.30", "reason": "Section XVI note 2(a): a motor of heading 8501 stays in its own heading even when dedicated to a scanner"}, {"code": "8501.10.60", "reason": "applies to motors of 18.65 W or more; bar code scanner motors are typically only a few watts"}, {"code": "8501.31", "reason": "DC motors over 37.5 W; output far lower"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00184", "reference_source": "ruling:M87234", "product": "a men's 100% cotton, jersey knit pullover with a V-neckline, long sleeves with tubular cuffs, and a tubular knit bottom", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling M87234, gold code 6110.20.2067:\nIn your letter dated October 16, 2006, you requested a tariff classification ruling on behalf of Supreme International. Your samples have been retained for our files. Style Number: 47SG3005 Garment Description: Men’s 70% cotton, 30% nylon, jersey knit pullover, neckband of rib and tubular knit construction, long sleeves with rib knit cuffs, rib knit bottom. HTSUS Chapter 61 Statistical Note 3 Stitch Count: 15 stitches per 2 centimeters HTSUS Chapter 61 Statistical Note 6 Stitch Count: 15 stitches per 2 centimeters Component Construction: All components are knit to shape and assembled by linking and looping. The front panel has a contoured V-neckline and full fashion marks at the armholes. The back panel has a contoured neckline and full fashion marks at the armholes and shoulders. The sleeves have full fashion marks at the armholes. The neckband has a self-start bottom and self-finished sides. HTSUS Number: 6110.20.2067 Textile Category Number: 338 To view the tariff language and current duty rates that correspond to these HTSUS provisions, please refer to the text of the most recent HTSUS, which is available on the World Wide Web at http://www.usitc.gov/tata/hts/. With the exception of certain products of China, quota/visa requirements are no longer applicable for merchandise which is the product of World Trade Organization (WTO) member countries. The textile category number above applies to merchandise produced in non-WTO member-countries. Quota and visa requirements are the result of international agreements that are subject to frequent renegotiations and changes. To obtain the most current information on quota and visa requirements applicable to this merchandise, we suggest you check, close to the time of shipment, the “Textile Status Report for Absolute Quotas” which is available on our web site at www.cbp.gov. For current information regarding possible textile safeguard actions on goods from China and related issues, we refer you to the web site of the Office of Textiles and Apparel of the Department of Commerce at otexa.ita.doc.gov. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in 19 CFR 177.9(b)(1) which states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in connection with the ruling request and incorporated therein, either directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. The merchandise, in its condition as imported into the United States, should conform to the facts and the description as set forth both in the ruling request and in this ruling. In the event that the facts or merchandise are modified in any way, this should be brought to the attention of Customs officials. The issuance of this ruling does not preclude periodic verification by Customs at the time of importation. A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, contact National Import Specialist Mary Ryan at 646-733-3271. Sincerely, Robert B. Swierupski, Director National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6110.20.20.69", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 6110 covers knitted pullovers; the tightened (tubular knit) bottom excludes heading 6109 (chapter 61 note 5) and 6105/6106 shirts", "GRI 6: 6110.20 of cotton; 6110.20.20 other (not 36% or more flax); not a sweater (fine jersey knit, assumed over 9 stitches per 2 cm), not a sweatshirt or vest; men's or boys' other; not described as knit to shape, so suffix .69 (338)"], "rationale": "A men's knit pullover with a tubular knit bottom is excluded from 6109 by chapter 61 note 5 and falls in heading 6110; being 100% cotton, it goes to 6110.20.20. NY N297115 (in force) classified a men's cut-and-sewn 100% cotton finely knit pullover with a rib knit bottom in 6110.20.2069, men's or boys' other. Assuming this jersey pullover is not a sweater and not knit to shape, the same line applies.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6110.20.20.10", "reason": "sweater line (345); applies only if stitch count and construction make it a sweater (9 or fewer stitches per 2 cm)"}, {"code": "6110.20.20.67", "reason": "only for knit-to-shape articles described in statistical note 6; jersey knit pullover assumed cut and sewn"}, {"code": "6109.10", "reason": "chapter 61 note 5 excludes garments with a ribbed or tightened bottom from heading 6109"}, {"code": "6110.20.20.41", "reason": "sweatshirt line; no fleece/napped surface described"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00185", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a Gearbox Assembly for Fertilizer Spreaders", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8432.90.0060):\nThe Gearbox Assembly, identified as Gleason Part Number 9310A, is specifically designed for use in manufacturing various models of fertilizer spreaders. It functions to convert the horizontal motion of the spreader's wheels and axle into vertical motion to drive a spinner disc for distributing granular fertilizer. The assembly includes components such as pinion gears, drive gears, nylon bushings, and a shaft that rotates the disc and agitator pin, which are integral to the operation of the fertilizer spreader. \n\nThe classification under HTSUS 8432.90.0060 is appropriate because it pertains to parts of agricultural machinery, specifically for fertilizer spreaders, as defined in heading 8432. The ruling confirms that the gearbox assembly does not qualify under subheading 8483.40.5010 due to the presence of additional components that do not transmit power. Furthermore, the assembly meets the criteria for duty-free treatment under subheading 9817.00.60, provided it is used in accordance with the actual use regulations and the necessary documentation is maintained. However, the primary classification is under 8432.90.0060, which is confirmed as the correct HTS code for this product.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8483.40.50.10", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8483 names gear boxes and other speed changers", "Section XVI note 2(a): a part that is itself a good of heading 8483 is classified there, not as a part of the spreader in 8432.90", "GRI 6: 8483.40 gear boxes and speed changers; fixed ratio speed changers ... selected by manual manipulation; 8483.40.50 other (not for paper machinery); suffix .10 fixed ratio speed changers (assumed fixed-ratio spreader gearbox)"], "rationale": "A gearbox assembly is a 'gear box or other speed changer' named in heading 8483, and Section XVI note 2(a) keeps such a good in its own heading even when made for fertilizer spreaders. Spreader gearboxes are fixed-ratio bevel-gear units, so the line is 8483.40.50.10. NY N355530 (in force) classified a fixed 1:1 ratio bevel gearbox for a specific machine application in 8483.40.5010.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8432.90.00", "reason": "Section XVI note 2(a) sends goods of heading 8483 to their own heading even when they are dedicated spreader parts; H348950 used 8432.90 only for components that are not goods of a specific heading"}, {"code": "8483.40.70.00", "reason": "other speed changers (e.g., automatically variable); a spreader gearbox is a fixed-ratio unit"}, {"code": "8483.40.50.80", "reason": "residual line; a fixed-ratio gearbox falls in .10"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00186", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a battery-operated sound reproducing apparatus designed for hunting that can reproduce various predator calls", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8519.81.3020):\nThe product in question, referred to as The Kat Attakk, is a battery-operated sound reproducing apparatus specifically designed for hunting purposes. It is characterized by its unique design resembling a cougar skull and its capability to reproduce 30 different sounds, each lasting between 10-16 minutes. The sounds are intended to attract predators, making it a specialized sound reproducing device. \n\nAccording to the Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), the applicable subheading for this type of merchandise is 8519.81.3020, which covers \"Sound recording or reproducing apparatus: Other apparatus: Using magnetic, optical or semiconductor media: Sound reproducing only: Other: Other.\" The classification is justified as the device primarily functions to reproduce sound, and the inclusion of a remote control does not alter its essential character as a sound reproducing apparatus. Furthermore, the ruling confirms that the device is imported as a set, with the sound reproducing component being the primary element of the set. The rate of duty for this classification is free, as indicated in the ruling.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8519.81.30.20", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8519 covers sound recording or reproducing apparatus; the device reproduces stored sounds", "Chapter 92 (heading 9208 decoy calls) covers mouth-blown/mechanical calls, and heading 9507 hunting requisites does not name electronic sound apparatus; the electrical sound-reproducing function is specifically described in 8519 (GRI 3(a) specificity)", "GRI 6: 8519.81 using semiconductor media (stored digital sounds, assumed); sound reproducing only; not a cassette player; 8519.81.30 other; suffix .20 other (not an optical disc player)"], "rationale": "A battery-operated device that plays back stored predator calls is a sound reproducing apparatus of heading 8519; the electrical playback function is more specifically described there than as a decoy call of 9208 or a hunting requisite of 9507. With sounds on semiconductor media and no recording capability, it falls in 'sound reproducing only: other', 8519.81.30.20. NY N334310 (in force) shows CBP placing a small battery-operated sound device in 8519.81, at .41.50 because that device also recorded.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8519.81.41.50", "reason": "applies to apparatus that also records; this device only reproduces pre-loaded calls (N334310's device recorded, hence .41)"}, {"code": "9208.90.00", "reason": "decoy calls of heading 9208 are non-electrical (mouth-blown) instruments; the device is an electrical sound reproducer"}, {"code": "9507.90", "reason": "hunting requisites heading yields to the more specific description of the electronic sound reproducing apparatus in 8519"}, {"code": "8518.22", "reason": "a loudspeaker alone would not store or reproduce sounds"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00188", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "sucker rods and sucker rod couplings", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 8413.91.90):\nThe subject merchandise, sucker rods and sucker rod couplings, are classified under HTSUS heading 8413, which covers \"Pumps for liquids, whether or not fitted with a measuring device; liquid elevators; parts thereof.\" The specific subheading 8413.91.90 pertains to \"Parts: Of pumps: Other.\" \n\nThe analysis begins with the definition and use of sucker rods, which are solid rods designed to connect surface oilwell pumping equipment to down-hole pumps, generating the necessary force to displace oil from below ground to the surface. The couplings are specifically designed to work with these rods, allowing for secure connections and capable of handling high torque requirements in deep wells.\n\nThe classification aligns with the Explanatory Notes (EN) to heading 8413, which explicitly includes sucker rods as parts of pumps for displacing liquids. The ruling also references a previous New York Ruling Letter (NY) R00333, which classified similar merchandise under the same subheading, reinforcing the consistency of this classification.\n\nMoreover, the couplings are not classified under heading 7307 (tube or pipe fittings) because they are not intended for use with tubes or pipes but are specifically designed for solid sucker rods. Therefore, both the sucker rods and their couplings are correctly classified under HTSUS subheading 8413.91.90, which is consistent with the definitions and intended use of the products.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8413.91.90.31", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: first-named article (sucker rods) classified; couplings classified separately", "Section XVI note 2(b): parts suitable for use solely or principally with reciprocating pumps for liquids of 8413.50 are classified in 8413.91 as parts of pumps", "GRI 6: 8413.91.90 parts of pumps, other; of subheading 8413.50.00 (reciprocating positive displacement pumps); suffix .31 sucker rods of a kind described in statistical note 3 to chapter 84"], "rationale": "Sucker rods link the surface pumping unit to the down-hole reciprocating pump and are used solely with those pumps, so under Section XVI note 2(b) they are parts of pumps of 8413.50 classified in 8413.91.90. HQ H310618 (in force) addressed sucker rods and couplings that had been liquidated under 8431.43.80 and considered the protestant's claim under 8413.91.90, noting CBP's earlier NY R00333 placing similar rods there; the full holding was truncated in the tool output, so the ruling is relied on for the analysis framework. The current tree has a dedicated line for sucker rods, 8413.91.90.31.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8431.43.80.60", "reason": "H310618 addressed entries liquidated as parts of boring machinery; sucker rods operate the well pump, not drilling machinery"}, {"code": "7228.30 / 7215", "reason": "rods are finished, threaded, dedicated pump parts, not merely steel bars"}, {"code": "8413.91.90.39", "reason": "residual line for other parts of 8413.50 pumps; sucker rods have their own statistical line (.31); sucker rod couplings would fall here"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00192", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "3M High Efficiency Particulate Filter (HE) GVP-440", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 9020.00.9000):\nThe 3M High Efficiency Particulate Filter (HE) GVP-440 is designed specifically for use with the GVP-Series Powered Air Purifying Respirator (PAPR), which classifies it as a breathing appliance under Chapter 90 of the Harmonized Tariff Schedule (HTSUS). The filter is not interchangeable with other types of respirators, indicating its specialized use. \n\nInitially, the user suggested classifying the filter under subheading 8421.39.8090, which pertains to filtering or purifying machinery and apparatus for gases. However, the Customs Laboratory analysis determined that the filter is primarily composed of non-woven glass fibers, which aligns with heading 7019, HTSUS. \n\nDespite this, Chapter 90, Note 1(e) does not exclude goods classified under heading 7019, allowing the filter to be classified under Chapter 90. The filter is identified as suitable for use solely with respirators of Chapter 90, and thus, falls under the provisions for parts and accessories of breathing appliances. \n\nThe applicable subheading for the 3M High Efficiency Particulate Filter (HE) GVP-440 is therefore 9020.00.9000, which covers other breathing appliances and gas masks, excluding protective masks without mechanical parts or replaceable filters. The duty rate for this classification is set at 2.5 percent ad valorem.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "5911.90.00.80", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: candidate headings 8421 (filtering apparatus for gases), 9020 (breathing appliances and parts) and 5911 (textile articles for technical uses, filtering)", "Section XVI note 1(e): Section XVI does not cover articles of textile material for technical uses of heading 5911, so a textile-media filter is excluded from 8421; chapter 90 note 2 would in any event send a filter to its own heading rather than 9020", "Chapter 59 note on heading 5911: textile articles for technical uses such as filtering; GRI 6: 5911.90.00 other; suffix .80 other"], "rationale": "The GVP-440 is a replaceable particulate filter for a 3M powered air purifying respirator. NY N253050 (in force) classified a 3M respirator particulate filter cartridge in 5911.90.0080, holding that Section XVI note 1(e) excludes textile technical filtering articles from heading 8421. Following that precedent for a like 3M filter, the line is 5911.90.00.80; if the medium proves non-textile, 8421.39 would apply instead.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8421.39", "reason": "Section XVI note 1(e) excludes textile technical articles of 5911 (N253050 rejected 8421.39 for a 3M respirator filter on this ground); would apply only if the media is not textile (e.g., glass fiber paper)"}, {"code": "9020.00.90", "reason": "parts of breathing appliances; a filter that is a good of another specific heading is not classified as a part under chapter 90 note 2"}, {"code": "6307.90", "reason": "made-up textile articles yield to 5911 for technical filtering articles"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00193", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "a SpeedGlas Welding Helmet with Respirator", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 9020.00.6000):\nThe product in question is a composite good consisting of a molded plastic helmet/face plate, a transparent liquid crystal display visor, and a respirator system. The helmet serves as a protective shell but does not seal the face from ambient air. The visor protects the welder's eyes from bright light during welding, while the respirator includes a battery-powered fan that filters ambient air and delivers it to the helmet, thereby protecting the welder's respiratory system from harmful fumes and sparks. \n\nThe ruling determined that the respirator is a significant component of the product, both in terms of cost and function, and is the largest and heaviest part of the assembly. The Harmonized System Explanatory Notes to General Rule of Interpretation 3(c) indicate that the classification should be based on the component that provides the essential character of the composite good. Since the respirator is classified under HTS 9020, which covers other breathing appliances and gas masks, it was determined that the entire product should be classified under this heading. Therefore, the applicable HTS code for the SpeedGlas Welding Helmet with Respirator is 9020.00.6000, with a duty rate of 2.5% ad valorem.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9020.00.60.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: prima facie classifiable in 6506.10 (safety headgear), 9004.90 (protective eye goggles) and 9020 (other breathing appliances); chapter 65 note 1 does not exclude it", "GRI 3(a): each heading describes only part of the composite good, so the headings are regarded as equally specific", "GRI 3(b): the powered respirator (blower, filter, air delivery to the headtop) gives the essential character by value, complexity and its role in protecting the user from welding fumes, so heading 9020", "GRI 6: 9020.00.60.00 other breathing appliances and gas masks (not underwater breathing devices)"], "rationale": "The Speedglas helmet with respirator combines safety headgear, an eye-protective filter and a powered air purifying respirator, so it is a composite good classified by GRI 3(b). The respirator function gives the essential character, placing it in heading 9020 as another breathing appliance, 9020.00.60.00. NY I80102 (in force) confirms that air purifying respirators with replaceable filters fall in 9020.00.6000; no ruling on this exact combined helmet was found.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6506.10.60", "reason": "safety headgear heading covers only the helmet shell; the powered respirator supplies the essential character (GRI 3(b))"}, {"code": "9004.90.00", "reason": "the auto-darkening filter protects the eyes but does not give the article its essential character"}, {"code": "9020.00.90.00", "reason": "parts and accessories line; the system is a complete breathing appliance"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00196", "reference_source": "ruling:N334395", "product": "clear polyethylene plastic bags without handles", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N334395, gold code 3923.21.0095:\n042 RE: The tariff classification of polyethylene bags from China Dear Mr. Liu: In your letter dated August 1, 2023, you requested a tariff classification ruling on behalf of your client, The Pack America Corp. The products under consideration are clear polyethylene bags. The bags are composed of 27-micron low-density polyethylene. They do not have handles. The bags will be imported in three sizes: 12 inches x 18 inches, 15 inches x 18 inches and 20 inches x 25 inches and will be packaged 100 bags/cardboard header. The bags are intended for use as packaging for various non-food related items. The applicable subheading for the polyethylene bags will be 3923.21.0095, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for articles for conveyance of packing of goods, of plastics: sacks and bags (including cones): of polymers of ethylene: other: other. The general rate of duty will be 3 percent ad valorem. Pursuant to U.S. Note 20 to Subchapter III, Chapter 99, HTSUS, products of China classified under subheading 3923.21.0095, HTSUS, unless specifically excluded, are subject to an additional 25 percent ad valorem rate of duty. At the time of importation, you must report the Chapter 99 subheading, i.e., 9903.88.03, in addition to subheading 3923.21.0095, HTSUS, listed above. The HTSUS is subject to periodic amendment, so you should exercise reasonable care in monitoring the status of goods covered by the Note cited above and the applicable Chapter 99 subheading. For background information regarding the trade remedy initiated pursuant to Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974, including information on exclusions and their effective dates, you may refer to the relevant parts of the USTR and CBP websites, which are available at https://ustr.gov/issue-areas/enforcement/section-301-investigations/tariff-actions and https://www.cbp.gov/trade/remedies/301-certain-products-china, respectively. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/current. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Christina Allen at julie.c.allen@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, Steven A. Mack Director National Commodity Specialist Division", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3923.21.00.95", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 3923 covers articles for the conveyance or packing of goods of plastics, including sacks and bags", "GRI 6: 3923.21 of polymers of ethylene; not reclosable with integral extruded closure; not retail carrier bags with handles (no handles); assumed a side exceeds 75 mm; suffix .95 other"], "rationale": "Clear polyethylene bags are sacks and bags for the conveyance or packing of goods of heading 3923 and, being of a polymer of ethylene, fall in 3923.21. Without handles, a reclosable extruded closure or a very small size, the residual statistical line 3923.21.00.95 applies. NY N015142 (in force) classified unprinted polyethylene bags without handles in 3923.21.0095.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3923.21.00.85", "reason": "polyethylene retail carrier bags with handles; these bags have no handles"}, {"code": "3923.21.00.30", "reason": "reclosable bags with integral extruded (zipper) closure; none stated"}, {"code": "3923.21.00.80", "reason": "bags with no side exceeding 75 mm; typical bags are larger"}, {"code": "3923.29", "reason": "the bags are of polyethylene, a polymer of ethylene"}]}}
{"item_id": "atlas_test_00197", "reference_source": "atlas_reasoning", "product": "optical assemblies for the ABI Prism 3100 Genetic Analyzer", "cbp_reference": "Reasoning summarized from the CBP ruling (gold code 9002.19.0000):\nThe optical assemblies in question consist of the upper lens assembly (product number 628-3537) and the lower lens assembly (product number 628-3538), both of which are designed to focus laser light and are integral components of the ABI Prism 3100 Genetic Analyzer. According to the ruling, these assemblies are classified as mounted lenses, which are specifically mentioned in Chapter 90, Note 2 (a) of the Harmonized Tariff Schedule. The applicable HTS subheading 9002.19.0000 covers lenses, prisms, mirrors, and other optical elements that are mounted and serve as parts or fittings for instruments and apparatus. Furthermore, the ruling confirms that the rate of duty for these items is 2.3 percent ad valorem, reinforcing their classification under this specific HTS code.", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "9027.90.56.50", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: the genetic analyzer is an electrical instrument for physical or chemical analysis using optical radiations of heading 9027 (9027.50.40)", "Chapter 90 note 2(b): parts suitable for use solely or principally with a particular instrument are classified with that instrument's parts provision (9027.90), since the assembly as a whole is not a good of a specific other heading (note 2(a))", "GRI 6: 9027.90 parts and accessories; of electrical instruments; other (not printed circuit assemblies); of instruments of subheading 9027.50; suffix .50 of articles of subheading 9027.50.40"], "rationale": "The ABI Prism 3100 is an electrical fluorescence-based analysis instrument of 9027.50.40, and an optical assembly dedicated to it is a part classified under chapter 90 note 2(b) in 9027.90. NY G86134 (in force) classified the excitation optics assembly for this same analyzer in former 9027.90.5450, parts of articles of 9027.50.40. In the current tree that line is 9027.90.56.50.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "9027.90.64.00", "reason": "parts of optical (non-electrical) instruments; the analyzer is an electrical instrument"}, {"code": "9013.20", "reason": "chapter 90 note 2(a) would apply only if the assembly were a laser alone; the assembly is a multi-component optical sub-system dedicated to the analyzer"}, {"code": "9027.90.54.00", "reason": "covers parts of electrophoresis instruments not incorporating an optical or other measuring device; the 3100 uses optical fluorescence detection"}]}}
